;a string with the name of the carcarName "­gcw-alfav10.png­Alfa Romeo 8C 1935"year 1935;displacement in litresdisplacement 3.882

builtIn 1
;---aerodynamics;the car's frontal air resitance.;this equals cw*S, where cw is the coefficient of friction ;and S is the frontal area of the car in square meters.;A modern sports car might have values as low as 0.6 for thisfrontAirResistance 0.35;air resistance of the side and top areas of the car.;these values are not that important to be very exact.sideAirResistance 2.8topAirResistance 2.5;used to simulate spoilers;negative values are downforce, positive values are upforcefrontLift 0.0rearLift 0.0;---chassis;Car's mass in kgmass 955;Car's momentum of inertia around the x,y and z axis.;Meassured in kg*m^2;For those not into physics that much, inertia specifies the car's;initial resistance to get rotationally accelerated around it's axis. Smaller, lighter;cars have much lower values here. A formula-1 car might have an inertia;around the y-axis af about 750 kg*m^2, whereas a big station wagon may have;4000 kg*m^2. lower values result in better cornering abilities;
; note that if you consider the car as a block of dimensions W H L
; Ix = mass x ( H^2 + L^2) / 12
; Iy = mass x ( W^2 + L^2) / 12
; Iz = mass x ( W^2 + H^2) / 12
;
inertia {1350, 1350, 200};The car's center of gravity.;This vector (as all the others that may follow) uses the following;coordinate system: X points to the right, Y points up and Z points to the front.;Coordinates are in meters.;The lower the CG, the better the cars handling.;Also, the further the CG is to the rear of the car, the more the car will tend;to oversteer in corners, making it much harder to handle. If your car uncontrollably;spins out in the rear a lot, try moving the CG further to the front.massCenter {0.0, -0.10, 0.30};An approximation of the car's borders, used for collision detection.;More than two boxes can be used, but if too many are used, performance ;may degrade. Each box is defined by 8 edges (top/front/right to bottom/rear/left).;number of collision boxesnumCollBoxes 1;a list of the collision boxes used, in this case only one# 0coll.tfr {0.58, 0.60, 2.00}coll.tfl {-0.58, 0.60, 2.00}coll.trr {0.28, 0.60, -2.12}coll.trl {-0.28, 0.60, -2.12}coll.bfr {0.58, -0.46, 2.00}coll.bfl {-0.58, -0.46, 2.00}coll.brr {0.28, -0.46, -2.12}coll.brl {-0.28 , -0.46, -2.12}

